= Framing effect — Simple =

A '''framing effect''' happens when the way information is presented changes how people judge or choose, even when the basic facts are the same.

“90% survive” and “10% die” describe the same rate, but they can feel different.

Frames can emphasize gains or losses, good features or bad features, or the benefits of acting versus the costs of not acting.

Not every wording difference is a framing effect. Sometimes wording actually changes the meaning of a question.

